<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yusuf IV of Granada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1432">1432</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ibn al-Mawl · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yusuf IV, known as Ibn al-Mawl, was Nasrid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> for a few months in 1432. Backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-castile">John II of Castile</a> after the Castilian victory at La Higueruela, he took power from Muhammad IX and ruled as a Castilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, but he soon lost support and was killed when Muhammad IX regained the throne. His brief reign showed how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> exploited Nasrid divisions.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yusuf ibn al-Mawl was not of the Nasrid male line. His mother was a daughter of Sultan Muhammad VI, and his father, Muhammad ibn al-Mawl, belonged to the Banū l-Mawl, an influential family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> that had backed the dynasty's founder and in 1309 helped overthrow Muhammad III. Chroniclers called him very old at his death, which suggests he was born in the 1360s. He appears in the record only in 1431, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> was in crisis after Muhammad IX had his rival Muhammad VIII executed and Castilian forces ravaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vega-of-granada">Vega of Granada</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Muhammad IX's opponents, led by the Bannigash family and above all Ridwan Bannigaš, chose Yusuf although other princes, among them the future Yusuf V and Sa'd, had stronger claims. Ridwan appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-castile">John II of Castile</a>, who was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Yusuf joined the Castilian army in the Vega, denounced Muhammad IX as a usurper and offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>. After the victory at La Higueruela on 1 July 1431, John II withdrew without securing Muhammad's submission, but ordered frontier commanders such as Diego Gómez de Ribera and Luis de Guzmán, Master of Calatrava, to help the pretender.</p><p>At Ardales on 16 September 1431 Yusuf signed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> agreement, which survives in Arabic and Castilian. Towns declared for him, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archidona">Archidona</a> and Iznájar surrendered in December, and on 31 December his supporters seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> as Muhammad IX fled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yusuf took power on 1 January 1432 and ratified the treaty on 27 January. He pledged lifelong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, a yearly tribute of 20,000 gold dinars, military help, attendance at the Castilian court, the release of all Christian captives and a ban on Christian conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The release of captives, worth labour and ransom to many families, caused particular hardship, and perpetual vassalage offended Islamic political norms.</p><p>Muhammad IX regrouped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> and Málaga and sought support from the Hafsid ruler Abu Faris and from Alfonso V of Aragon. His nephew Yusuf ibn Ahmad entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> with about a thousand men and besieged Yusuf IV in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>. Castilian help was slow and brief, and the citizens invited Muhammad IX back. Found hiding in the palace after several days, Yusuf was executed in April 1432, and his Bannigash supporters fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. He may be the Abenamar of the frontier ballad Romance of Abenamar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yusuf IV of Granada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
